            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life in Russia</strong><br>\nIgor Stravinsky was born on June 17, 1882, in Oranienbaum, Russia. Despite his parents' expectations for him to study law, Stravinsky was drawn to music at an early age. He started piano lessons as a young boy and soon began to compose his own pieces. Although he enrolled in university to study law, he continued to pursue music and started studying composition under Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov after his father's death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Compositions</strong><br>\nStravinsky's early work was marked by a strong influence from Russian folk music and the late Romantic style. His first significant success came with the ballet \"The Firebird,\" which premiered in 1910 and was acclaimed for its melodic ingenuity and use of orchestral color. This success established Stravinsky as a leading figure in ballet music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Rite of Spring and New Rhythms</strong><br>\nIn 1913, Stravinsky's \"The Rite of Spring\" was performed in Paris, causing a sensation and a near-riot in the audience due to its avant-garde structure, dissonance, and rhythm. This ballet challenged conventional expectations of music and dance, making Stravinsky a controversial and revolutionary figure in the classical music scene.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
