            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Life in Switzerland and France</strong><br>\nFollowing the outbreak of World War I and the Russian Revolution, Stravinsky lived in Switzerland and later moved to France. During these years, he experimented with different styles, including neoclassicism, which sought to return to the clarity and simplicity of earlier music forms. His works from this period include \"Pulcinella\" and \"Symphony of Psalms.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neoclassical Period</strong><br>\nStravinsky's neoclassical period spanned the 1920s to the 1950s, marked by a return to traditional structures and clarity of form. It was his reaction against the perceived complexity and excesses of late Romantic music. Works from this period show his use of older forms, such as the concerto grosso and fugue, with a modern twist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to the United States and New Ventures</strong><br>\nIn 1939, with Europe on the brink of World War II, Stravinsky moved to the United States, where he would live for the rest of his life. He continued to innovate, exploring serial techniques that employed a fixed series of notes to structure compositions. His American years were also characterized by a variety of works across different mediums, including film scores.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
