          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Serialist Approach</strong><br>\nStravinsky began to explore serialism more thoroughly in the 1950s after meeting with other composers like Arnold Schoenberg. This marked another significant shift in his compositional style. Works like \"Canticum Sacrum\" and \"Agon\" display his mastery of this complex method, demonstrating his versatility and willingness to adapt to new musical languages.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Teaching</strong><br>\nEven in his later years, Stravinsky remained active in the music community as a teacher and mentor to younger composers. He held a number of prestigious positions and was known for his articulate and insightful approach to music education. His influence extended well beyond his own compositions, shaping the future of music through his guidance and instruction.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Musical Legacy</strong><br>\nIgor Stravinsky passed away on April 6, 1971, in New York City. His death marked the end of an era in music composition. He is widely regarded as one of the most important and influential composers of the 20th century, having pushed the boundaries of musical design and thought with his innovative compositions and theories. His legacy continues to influence composers and musicians around the world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
